.PP
Super+Left and Super+Right snap only while \fB[multitasking] snap_windows\fR
is true; Super+Up and Super+Down still maximize, restore and minimize
without it.
.PP
Shortcuts use the key's position on the Latin layout, so they work the same
while another layout is active. A client that inhibits shortcuts (a remote
desktop or virtual machine window) receives all of them except Super+L,
Ctrl+Alt+Del and the virtual terminal keys.
.PP
In the window picker Tab and the arrow keys move, releasing Alt selects and
Esc cancels. In Task View the arrow keys move, Enter selects, Delete closes
the window and Esc leaves. After Move or Size from the window menu the
arrow keys move or resize the window (with Ctrl: by single pixels) until
Enter or Esc; a window being moved also follows the pointer until a click.
.SH POINTER, TOUCH AND GESTURES
Drag a title bar to move a window; drag it to a screen edge to snap it (top:
maximize; sides: halves; the outer quarter of a side: that corner), with a
preview of the result. Double-click a title bar to maximize; right-click it
for the window menu, which also sends the window to another monitor or
desktop. Drag the border or a corner to resize. Super+left drag
moves and Super+right drag resizes from anywhere on a window.
.PP
Resting the pointer on the maximize button opens the snap layouts: two
halves, a half beside two quarters, and four quarters. Clicking a zone puts
the window there. Dragging a window to the middle of a screen's top edge
opens the same layouts at the top of the screen; dropping the window on a
zone puts it there. \fB[multitasking]\fR turns each of these off.
.PP
On a touch screen a finger does what the pointer does on title bars,
buttons, the window picker and Task View. On a touchpad, three or four
fingers sideways go to the next or previous desktop, up opens Task View and
down shows the desktop (\fB[touchpad] gestures\fR). Pinch and hold gestures
are passed to applications. Drawing tablets and pens are supported with
pressure, tilt and their buttons.
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML files, later ones overriding earlier ones key by key:
.PP
.nf
/usr/share/boswas/compositor/compositor.toml   shipped defaults
/etc/boswas/compositor.toml                    the administrator
~/.config/boswas/compositor.toml               the user (written by Settings)
~/.config/boswas/appearance.toml               written by boswas\-preset
.fi
.PP
A file that does not parse, or that contains an unknown key, is reported and
ignored as a whole. \fIappearance.toml\fR may contain the [appearance] table
only. \fBboswas\-compositor \-\-send reload\-config\fR applies changes to a
running session.
.TP
.B [keyboard]
\fBlayout\fR, \fBvariant\fR, \fBmodel\fR, \fBoptions\fR (XKB names, for
example layout "us,in"); \fBrepeat_delay\fR (milliseconds),
\fBrepeat_rate\fR (per second); \fBnumlock\fR (Num Lock is on when the
session starts, and is turned on or off when the setting changes; a reload
otherwise keeps it as it is).
Accessibility: \fBsticky_keys\fR (Shift, Ctrl and Alt pressed and released on
their own stay down for the next key; Super keeps its own behaviour),
\fBsticky_keys_lock\fR (with sticky keys, a modifier pressed twice stays down
until it is pressed again; default true), \fBfilter_keys\fR (turns on the
two times that follow), \fBslow_keys_ms\fR (a key counts only after it was
held this long; 0, the default, or 100 to 5000) and \fBbounce_keys_ms\fR (a
key pressed again this soon after its release is ignored; 0 or 100 to 5000,
default 500).
.TP
.B [pointer]
Mice and trackballs: \fBnatural_scroll\fR, \fBaccel_speed\fR (\-1.0 to 1.0),
\fBaccel_profile\fR ("adaptive" or "flat"), \fBleft_handed\fR,
\fBcursor_theme\fR, \fBcursor_size\fR.
.TP
.B [touchpad]
\fBtap_to_click\fR, \fBnatural_scroll\fR, \fBaccel_speed\fR,
\fBdisable_while_typing\fR, \fBgestures\fR.
.TP
.B [output]
\fBscale\fR: 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75 or 2.0, for every output that has no
setting of its own. Not set (the shipped default): each output gets the
scale recommended for it, from the size its EDID reports and the
resolution it shows, as Windows recommends one (a 14-inch 1920x1080 panel
150 %, a 15.6-inch one 125 %, a 24-inch 1920x1080 monitor 100 %, a 27-inch
3840x2160 one 150 %), never leaving less than 1024x720 logical pixels; 100 %
where the size is unknown or implausible, more than 1.6 m wide, and on the
virtual graphics devices of virtual machines (virtio_gpu, qxl, bochs,
vmwgfx, vboxvideo, cirrus, hyperv_drm).
.TP
.BI [outputs. NAME ]
One monitor, by connector name (\fBeDP\-1\fR, \fBHDMI\-A\-1\fR; see
\fB\-\-send outputs\fR): \fBenabled\fR, \fBmode\fR ("1920x1080" or
"1920x1080@60", one of the modes the monitor offers; without a rate, the
highest it offers at that size; without a mode, its native size at its
highest rate), \fBscale\fR, \fBposition\fR ([x, y] of its top-left
corner in logical pixels), \fBrotation\fR ("normal", "90", "180" or
"270") and \fBadaptive_sync\fR (true: variable refresh rate, also known as
adaptive sync, where the monitor and its driver offer it; off by default;
never with the pixman renderer). Without a position,
monitors are placed side by side from left to right. The last enabled
monitor is never turned off.
.TP
.B [night_light]
\fBenabled\fR; \fBtemperature\fR (2500 to 6500 kelvin); \fBscheduled\fR with
\fBfrom\fR and \fBto\fR ("HH:MM", local time) turns it on by the clock.
Turned on or off in quick settings, it stays so until the schedule's next
start or end. Needs a graphics driver with gamma tables; where no monitor
has one, quick settings shows night light as not available.
.TP
.B [power]
\fBscreen_off_seconds\fR: time without input before the screens turn off (0:
never). Any input turns them on again.
.TP
.B [lock]
\fBidle_seconds\fR: time without input before the session locks (0: never,
if the policy allows it). \fBcommand\fR: the lock screen program.
\fBshow_weather\fR (default: true): the lock screen shows the weather of
the home place chosen in Weather, from the summary that
\fBboswas\-weather\fR(1) keeps in
\fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/boswas/weather.json\fR; it is not lock policy, so a
user's file may set it. The lock screen itself never contacts the
network.
.TP
.B [shell]
\fBcommand\fR: the shell program.
.TP
.B [input_method]
\fBcommand\fR: the input method program, for example ["fcitx5"]; empty for
none.
.PP
.RS
The three \fBcommand\fR keys are read from the first two files only: a
user's file cannot name these programs. The shell and the lock screen are
started as trusted clients. The input method is offered the input-method
and virtual-keyboard protocols only (it sees what is typed and types
itself) and none of the shell's other privileges.
.RE
.TP
.B [xwayland]
\fBenabled\fR: run an X server for X11 applications (default: true). A
user can turn it off; when the first two files turn it off, a user's file
does not turn it back on.
.TP
.B [apps]
\fBterminal\fR (Ctrl+Alt+T), \fBfiles\fR (Super+E), \fBsettings\fR
(Super+I) and \fBtask_manager\fR (Ctrl+Shift+Esc), each a program with its
arguments.
.TP
.B [appearance]
\fBscheme\fR ("dark" or "light"), \fBreduced_motion\fR (no animations),
\fBwallpaper\fR (a PNG or JPEG file shown by the shell instead of the
default background), \fBaccent\fR (the accent colour, a hue of the
palette: "iris", the default, "azure", "sky", "mint", "sun", "coral",
"orchid" or "slate"; the shell, the compositor's own surfaces and the Qt
applications take the same shades of it, moved where needed so that text on
it stays readable) and \fBtransparency\fR (default true; false turns glass
off: the shell's surfaces and the compositor's own are their opaque tints,
no blur is offered, and title bars have no Mica; see
.BR GLASS ).
.TP
.B [accessibility]
\fBtext_scale\fR (text size in percent, 100 to 225: the shell, the title
bars and the Qt applications draw their text that much larger and nothing
else; a title bar's text grows as far as its height allows) and
\fBalways_show_scrollbars\fR (Qt applications show full scroll bars instead
of thin lines that widen under the pointer).
.TP
.B [color_filter]
\fBenabled\fR; \fBfilter\fR: "grayscale" (the default), "inverted",
"grayscale_inverted", or a correction for colour blindness that moves the
differences a person cannot see where they can: "deuteranopia" and
"protanopia" (red-green), "tritanopia" (blue-yellow); \fBshortcut\fR
(Super+Ctrl+C turns the filter on and off). The whole picture is drawn
through the filter, which needs the OpenGL ES renderer: with pixman (see
\fBSTARTING ON REAL HARDWARE\fR) and on the headless backend colour filters
are not available. Screenshots and screen capture show the picture without
the filter.
.TP
.B [magnifier]
\fBenabled\fR (the screen under the pointer is enlarged), \fBzoom\fR (100
to 1600 percent; default 200) and \fBstep\fR (what Super+Plus and
Super+Minus add and take away: 25, 50, 100, the default, 150, 200 or 400).
The view follows the pointer, which stays where it is on the screen, and
never leaves the screen's edges. It works with both renderers. Screenshots
show the picture without magnification.
.TP
.B [taskbar]
Read by the shell: \fBalignment\fR ("center", the default, or "left"),
\fBauto_hide\fR (the taskbar reserves no room and stays out of sight but for
a sliver at the bottom edge, where the pointer brings it back),
\fBtask_view\fR (the Task View button; default true), \fBclock_seconds\fR,
\fBbadges\fR (a taskbar button shows how many notifications of its
application the notification centre holds; default true) and
\fBwidgets\fR (the widgets button at the left end, or beside the tray when
the group is at the left, with the weather of the lock screen's summary when
there is one; default true; Super+W opens the widgets board either way).
.TP
.B [desktop]
Read by the shell: \fBicons\fR (the files and folders of the desktop folder,
XDG_DESKTOP_DIR, as icons on the wallpaper; default true),
\fBsystem_icons\fR (besides them, from "home", "trash" and "computer", in
this order; default ["trash"]), \fBicon_size\fR ("small", "medium", the
default, or "large": 32, 48 or 96 pixels), \fBauto_arrange\fR (the icons
fill the grid from the top-left, column by column, in the order chosen
with Sort by; default false) and \fBalign_to_grid\fR (icons placed by hand
snap to the grid; default true). The desktop's View menu changes the last
three and \fBicons\fR in the user's file.
.TP
.B [start]
Read by the shell: \fBlayout\fR ("default": three rows of pinned
applications and three of recommendations; "more_pins": four and one;
"more_recommendations": two and four), \fBrecently_added\fR and
\fBmost_used\fR (what "Recommended" shows; both default true), and
\fBfolders\fR (buttons next to the power button, from "settings", "files",
"documents", "downloads", "music", "pictures" and "videos"; default
["settings", "files"]).
.TP
.B [multitasking]
\fBsnap_windows\fR (dragging a window to an edge snaps it, and
Super+Left/Right snap; default true), \fBsnap_layouts_on_maximize\fR and
\fBsnap_layouts_on_drag\fR (the snap layouts when the pointer rests on the
maximize button and when a window is dragged to the top of a screen; both
default true and need \fBsnap_windows\fR), \fBtaskbar_windows\fR and
\fBalt_tab_windows\fR ("desktop", the default: the windows of the current
desktop; "all": of every desktop, a window of another desktop is reached by
switching to it).
.TP
.B [notifications]
Read by the shell: \fBdo_not_disturb\fR (only critical notifications pop
up; the others go to the notification centre silently. Quick settings and
the notification centre switch it for the session, as they do night
light), \fBdo_not_disturb_scheduled\fR with \fBdo_not_disturb_from\fR and
\fBdo_not_disturb_to\fR ("HH:MM"; do not disturb turns on at the first time
and back to the setting at the second), \fBbanners_off\fR (names of
applications, as they call themselves, whose notifications do not pop up
but still reach the notification centre) and \fBtoast_seconds\fR (the
least time a notification stays on screen: 5, the default, 7, 15, 30, 60 or
300).
.TP
.B [clipboard]
Read by the shell: \fBhistory\fR (default true: the shell keeps the last 25
things copied, in memory, and Super+V shows them; false: Super+V offers to
turn it on, and only pinned items are kept) and \fBcleared_at\fR (a Unix
time in seconds, written by Settings for "Clear clipboard data": the shell
forgets what was copied before then, except pinned items, and empties the
clipboard if it holds such an item; default 0). See
.BR boswas\-shell (1).
.TP
.B [policy]
Read from the first two files only; ignored in a user's file.
\fBlock_idle_max_seconds\fR: the longest lock timeout a user may choose (0:
no limit). \fBprivileged_protocols\fR: "shell" (default) or "all" (see
SECURITY).
.SH X11 APPLICATIONS
With \fB[xwayland] enabled\fR the compositor of a desktop session starts
.BR Xwayland (1)
and manages its windows like any other: the same frame, snapping, picker
and Task View. \fBDISPLAY\fR is set for programs the session starts. The
clipboard is shared in both directions. X11 applications can see each
other's windows and input, as on any X server; they cannot see Wayland
applications. X11 windows are drawn at scale 1 and enlarged on a scaled
monitor.
.SH SECURITY
Ordinary clients get the application protocols only: they cannot read the
screen, other windows or global key strokes.
.PP
The privileged protocols (layer shell for panels and overlays, session
lock, the window list, clipboard control, input method and virtual
keyboard) are offered only to programs the compositor started itself, over
a connection it created for them. Clients that connect through a security
context (Flatpak and other sandboxes) never get them.
\fBprivileged_protocols = "all"\fR offers them to every client outside a
sandbox; it exists for development and must not be set on a managed device.
.PP
Nobody sees the screen without the user's say. The Print key and the
\fBcapture\fR command open the capture screen of the shell, where the user
chooses what to keep and confirms. A program that asks for pictures of the
screen itself (a screenshot tool, a screen recorder, the desktop portal
behind screen sharing in browsers and meetings; the wlr\-screencopy
protocol) waits until the shell has asked "Allow \fIprogram\fR to see your
screen?" and the user has allowed it. The permission covers that program
while it keeps capturing and ends a few seconds after its last picture; a
mark on the taskbar shows while any program may capture, and a click on it
stops them all. A refusal holds for twenty seconds. Sandboxed programs are
not offered the protocol, and nothing is captured while the session is
locked.
.PP
The compositor owns the session lock. Locking blanks every output and takes
input away from applications before the lock screen is started; only a lock
client's unlock request ends it. If the lock screen exits without
unlocking, the outputs stay blank and it is started again. The session is
also locked before the system suspends or hibernates
(\fI/usr/lib/systemd/system\-sleep/boswas\-lock\fR).
.PP
The compositor runs as the logged-in user. Device access comes from the
login session (libseat, logind); it has no setuid bit and needs no
capability.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas\-\fIWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fB.sock\fR accepts one JSON
object per line, for example {"cmd": "outputs"}. Connections to this socket
may ask for \fBversion\fR (with "color_filters": whether the renderer can
draw the colour filters), \fBlock\fR, \fBreload\-config\fR, \fBoutputs\fR
(every monitor with its modes, scale, "recommended_scale" (the scale it
uses when none is set), position, rotation and
"adaptive_sync": whether it offers variable refresh rate and whether it is
on) and
\fBcapture\fR (opens the capture screen; "mode": "screen" or "region").
The window list, window control, desktops, thumbnails, screenshots, the
answer to a program that asks to see the screen, \fBpaste\fR and \fBquit\fR
are available to the shell only, on the descriptor the compositor hands it.
\fBpaste\fR (the clipboard history and the emoji panel) gives the window
that has the keyboard the key stroke that pastes there, Ctrl+V or, in a
terminal (by its application id), Ctrl+Shift+V, once the keys still held
are let go; it names no keys, and does nothing while the session is locked
or when no window has the keyboard. \fBnight\-light\fR with "active" turns
night light on or off now (with a schedule, until its next change); its
answer and the night-light event say "enabled", "active", "override" (a
choice against the schedule holds) and "available" (a monitor can be
tinted). \fBproject\fR with "mode" ("pc\-only", "duplicate", "extend",
"second\-only") uses the monitors so for the session, over the
\fB[outputs.\fINAME\fB]\fR settings; \fBprojection\fR says how many are
connected and how they are used.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOSWAS_LOG
Log filter (for example \fBdebug\fR, \fBboswas_compositor=trace\fR or
\fBinfo,libseat=debug\fR). Messages go to standard error. With
\fBinfo,boswas_compositor::stats=debug\fR each output that draws reports
once a second how many frames it drew, how long they took and how much of
the screen they drew again: the first thing to look at when the desktop
feels slow.
.TP
.B BOSWAS_RENDERER
How the \fBudev\fR backend draws: \fBauto\fR (the default: OpenGL ES on a
hardware renderer, pixman otherwise), \fBgles\fR or \fBpixman\fR.
.TP
.B XCURSOR_THEME
Cursor theme when \fBpointer.cursor_theme\fR is empty.
.TP
.B GLIBC_TUNABLES
When the session does not set it, the shell, the lock screen and the login
screen are started with \fBglibc.malloc.mmap_threshold=262144\fR, so that the
shell gives large buffers back to the system once it lets them go; other
programs do not get it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended as asked: a login succeeded on the login screen, or the
user signed out.
.TP
.B 1
Any error that is none of the following.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
The compositor could not start: no usable seat, no graphics device within
the wait, or (login screen only) no output.
.TP
.B 4
The login screen kept failing and the compositor gave up. A desktop
session never ends this way.
.PP
A failure is explained by one error line on standard error. Statuses 3 and
4 mean that starting the compositor again would end the same way;
.B boswas\-greeter
then shows the text login.
